ingress-nginx is exposed as a LoadBalancer, which on ferry means the Mac's own
address answers for it. Find it with:

  kubectl -n ingress-nginx get svc ingress-nginx-controller

An ingress controller asks for ports 80 and 443, and those are below 1024, so
the Mac will not let an ordinary process listen on them. Unless ferry-proxy is
running as root -- which is what

  FERRY_HOST_CLUSTER_IPS=1 ferry up

arranges -- the EXTERNAL-IP stays <pending> and the reason is recorded on the
Service itself, visible with `kubectl describe svc -n ingress-nginx
ingress-nginx-controller`.

The node ports work either way, and are enough to test an Ingress:

  kubectl -n ingress-nginx get svc ingress-nginx-controller \
    -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[?(@.port==80)].nodePort}'
  curl -H 'Host: your.host' http://<mac>:<that port>/

Then point an Ingress at a Service. Host-based rules need the name to resolve,
so either use the address directly with a Host header or add a line to
/etc/hosts.
